  "metadata": {
    "title": "Public element sets against reconstructed orbits through the storms of 2024: what was measured, what was corrected, and what it does not show",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22656992",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-08",
    "description": "<p><strong>This version is superseded by v2: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22661809\">10.5281/zenodo.22661809</a>.</strong> Version 2 corrects manoeuvre exclusions, horizon criteria and covariance coordinates, and adds the completed September calendar replay and topocentric comparison while retaining both learned-propagator negative findings. Please use v2 for the current results.</p>\n<p><strong>Original v1 description (superseded):</strong> Public SGP4 element sets are compared with reconstructed orbits for fifteen spacecraft at 460 to 1,338 km over a quiet window and three storm windows, with 1,201 usable sets and laser ranging as a further reference. The paper reports prediction horizons, covariance calibration, observed-ap storm-term skill, the held-out dSGP4 evaluation, post-burn fit errors, radio horizons, and convention and provenance faults exposed by independent checks. These are measurements on the stated population, not operational guarantees.</p>",
